Use ASP.NET Core's `WebApplicationFactory<T>` — it hosts your app in-memory and gives you an `HttpClient` that talks to it without opening a real port.

```csharp
// MyMcpServerTests.cs
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.Testing;
using System.Net.Http.Json;
using Xunit;

public class McpIntegrationTests : IClassFixture<WebApplicationFactory<Program>>
{
    private readonly HttpClient _client;

    public McpIntegrationTests(WebApplicationFactory<Program> factory)
    {
        _client = factory.CreateClient();
    }

    [Fact]
    public async Task Initialize_ReturnsCapabilities()
    {
        var request = new
        {
            jsonrpc = "2.0",
            id = 1,
            method = "initialize",
            @params = new
            {
                protocolVersion = "2025-03-26",
                capabilities = new { },
                clientInfo = new { name = "test-client", version = "1.0" }
            }
        };

        var response = await _client.PostAsJsonAsync("/mcp", request);

        response.EnsureSuccessStatusCode();
        var body = await response.Content.ReadFromJsonAsync<JsonElement>();
        Assert.Equal("2025-03-26",
            body.GetProperty("result")
                .GetProperty("protocolVersion")
                .GetString());
    }

    [Fact]
    public async Task ToolsCall_ExecutesTool()
    {
        var request = new
        {
            jsonrpc = "2.0",
            id = 2,
            method = "tools/call",
            @params = new
            {
                name = "my-tool",
                arguments = new { input = "hello" }
            }
        };

        var response = await _client.PostAsJsonAsync("/mcp", request);

        response.EnsureSuccessStatusCode();
        var body = await response.Content.ReadFromJsonAsync<JsonElement>();
        Assert.False(body.GetProperty("result")
                        .GetProperty("isError")
                        .GetBoolean());
    }
}
```

**Key points:**

1. **`WebApplicationFactory<Program>`** boots your entire middleware pipeline in-process — no port, no network.
2. Replace `Program` with your entry-point class (expose it via `public partial class Program { }` in `Program.cs` if needed).
3. Override services for isolation (e.g., mock a database) using `factory.WithWebHostBuilder(b => b.ConfigureServices(...))`.
4. For **SSE-based MCP** (Streamable HTTP), read the response as a stream and parse `text/event-stream` lines manually or with a helper.
5. Add `Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.Testing` NuGet to your test project.

This gives you full protocol-level coverage (JSON-RPC framing, headers, status codes) with the speed of an in-process call.
